INSERT INTO public.corpus_findings
  (area, topic, claim, basis, confidence, quantities, source)
VALUES
  ('volume-per-muscle','Weekly volume band - the anchor row','A systematic review categorizing weekly per-muscle volume as low (<12 sets), moderate (12-20), and high (>20) found moderate significantly superior to low for every muscle measured; high trended above moderate but reached statistical significance only for triceps. This is the single strongest, most-corroborated dose row in the corpus (appears independently in three videos across two channels) and is the reason a naive session cap is dangerous: it is the one finding that shows a real penalty, and the penalty is for dropping below 12 weekly sets.','systematic review / meta-analysis','strong','{"note": "<12 low, 12-20 moderate, >20 high weekly sets; significance for high-vs-moderate only in triceps"}'::jsonb,'Flow High Performance ''How Many Sets for Muscle Growth?'' + ''Is Junk Volume Real?''; House of Hypertrophy ''How Many Sets Per Muscle? We Examine the Science'' (2022 meta-analysis)'),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Weekly dose-response above 20 sets','A meta-analysis of 35 studies with 1,000+ subjects, using fractional set counting, found the best-fitting model was a square-root curve in which growth continued rising to 43 weekly sets per muscle with no plateau and no inverted-U, though later sets are estimated at roughly one-third the stimulus of early sets and credible intervals widen sharply above 25 weekly sets because few studies tested beyond that. A separate 67-study preprint meta-analysis reached the same 43-sets-per-week figure.','meta-analysis','strong','{"note": "43 weekly sets; ~1/3 stimulus for later sets; uncertainty widens above 25 weekly sets"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''More Sets, More Growth'' / ''How Many Sets per Workout?''; Menno Henselmans ''This is how to maximize muscle according to 67 studies'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Weekly ceiling - CONTESTED','Whether weekly volume above ~20 sets adds anything is genuinely contested: three studies using long rest (2-3 min) found no benefit past 12-18 weekly sets, while three using short rest (60-90s) plus a 12-week study in 31 trained men (22 vs 42 vs 52 weekly quad sets) found the highest volumes grew most. Henselmans separately contests the ''no inverted-U'' finding, noting subjects were college-aged and not in an energy deficit, i.e. maximum recovery capacity.','study synthesis','moderate','{"note": "12-18 weekly sets (long rest) vs 30-52 weekly sets (short rest / progressive)"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''The ULTIMATE Guide to How Many Sets'' / ''2.2x MORE Muscle''; Menno Henselmans 67-studies'),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Rest-interval moderator - CONTESTED and superseded','The earlier explanation for the high-vs-low weekly volume split - that short rest intervals require more sets - is contested by the newest evidence: the 35-study meta-analysis found the sets-to-hypertrophy relationship held similarly regardless of rest length, with the presenter stating ''the most recent evidence does not support'' the interaction. A moderator analysis inside that same meta found the opposite (plateau at lower sets with 3-min rest than 1-min), which the authors themselves call exploratory and inconclusive.','meta-analysis','weak','{"note": "1-min vs 3-min rest"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''How Many Sets per Workout?'' / ''More Sets, More Growth'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','High-volume fatigue cost','In the 12-week 22/42/52 weekly-quad-set study, the highest-volume group reported excessive perceived fatigue in the final weeks once volume exceeded 40 weekly sets, and the presenter explicitly declines to recommend anyone exceed ~40 weekly sets despite the growth data favoring it.','study','moderate','{"note": "40+ weekly sets"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''2.2x MORE Muscle: This New Study Is Surprising...'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Minimum effective and maintenance volume','The 67-study meta-analysis found a minimum effective weekly volume of 4 sets per muscle for growth; a 2017 meta-analysis found 9+ weekly sets beat fewer than 9 (mostly untrained subjects, data capped near 12 weekly sets); one coach estimates a maintenance-only floor around 8 weekly sets, explicitly as illustration rather than measurement.','meta-analysis','moderate','{"note": "MEV 4 weekly sets; 9+ > <9 weekly sets; ~8 weekly sets maintenance (opinion)"}'::jsonb,'Menno Henselmans 67-studies; House of Hypertrophy ''The ULTIMATE Guide''; Flow High Performance ''Complete Volume Guidelines'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Practical weekly convergence','Two presenters who each cite the 43-sets-per-week model independently land on 10-20 weekly sets per muscle as their actual recommendation once time, joint stress, injury risk, and data thinness above 25 sets are accounted for - one adding he is aware of two unpublished studies failing to find extra growth from very high volumes.','practice / expert synthesis','moderate','{"note": "10-20 weekly sets per muscle"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''More Sets, More Growth''; Menno Henselmans 67-studies'),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Per-session ceiling - THE central number, and its two opposite readings','The one meta-analysis that actually modeled sets-per-muscle-per-session produced the figure 11 sets/session - but the two channels covering it read it in opposite directions. House of Hypertrophy reports the same dataset showed NO clear sign of a plateau up to 20+ sets in a single session, with 11 being merely the point where credible intervals widen and data thins (versus 31 sets for the weekly equivalent). Henselmans presents 11 as the ''maximum productive volume'' plateau. Both agree the 11 figure comes from a statistical convention - the point where additional sets no longer have >50% probability of exceeding the smallest detectable effect - which Henselmans himself calls ''a rather arbitrary convention,'' not a biological wall.','meta-analysis (interpretation contested)','strong','{"note": "11 sets/session (confidence boundary); no plateau observed to 20+ sets/session; 31 sets/week equivalent"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''How Many Sets per Workout? - This NEW Study Is Epic''; Menno Henselmans ''This is the max volume you should do per workout'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Per-session ceiling - fatal confound','Henselmans, while advocating the session cap, openly flags that the per-session analysis he cites was NOT controlled for total weekly volume, so it cannot separate a true per-session ceiling from the effect of total weekly dose - he calls this ''messy'' and a matter for future research. No study in the entire corpus holds weekly volume fixed while varying only sets-per-session for one muscle and measures growth.','stated limitation of the cited meta-analysis','moderate',NULL,'Menno Henselmans ''This is the max volume you should do per workout''; corroborated by synthesis across House of Hypertrophy and Jeff Nippard batches'),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Per-session ceiling - the 6-set figure and why it is weak','The strictest session number, 6 sets per muscle per workout, comes from an unofficial, non-peer-reviewed meta-analysis by James Krieger. Its low value is at least partly an artifact: the analysis mixed trained and untrained subjects, and untrained subjects rarely exceeded 6 sets per session, biasing the low end, and it may be confounded by total weekly sets rather than isolating a session effect. Jeff Nippard, who popularizes the 6-set threshold, concedes it reflects ''the average trend'' with ''a lot of variance'' and that some individuals see no junk volume until 10, 12, or more sets per session.','unpublished meta-analysis with acknowledged confounds','moderate','{"note": "6 sets/session (Krieger, unpublished, 9 studies) vs 10-12+ for high responders"}'::jsonb,'Menno Henselmans ''This is the max volume you should do per workout''; Jeff Nippard ''Junk Volume: Why You Should Avoid It''; House of Hypertrophy ''Comparing Training Splits (20 STUDIES)'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Per-session diminishing returns - and the prescribed fix','A meta-regression found significant diminishing returns beyond roughly 10 sets for a muscle within one workout, but the presenter''s prescribed remedy is explicitly redistribution, not reduction: the worked example is that 12 chest sets in one session would likely be slightly better organized as 6 sets twice per week. Another coach''s rule is that above ~10 sets in a session, splitting across the week probably helps meaningfully, while below ~10 added frequency probably does little. This is the single most directly actionable per-session finding in the corpus, and it prescribes moving volume rather than deleting it.','meta-analysis + coach heuristic','strong','{"note": "~10 sets/session trigger; 12-in-one ? 6-twice example"}'::jsonb,'Flow High Performance ''Are You Leaving Gains on the Table? (Yes)'' and ''How Many Times per Week Should You Train Each Muscle?'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Per-session ceiling - CONTRARY RCT (bro-split won)','Bartolome and colleagues had a bro-split group train quadriceps with roughly 20 sets in a single session (2-min rest) against a full-body group performing the same 15-20 weekly sets across 3-4 sessions; over 10 weeks the bro-split group''s vastus lateralis and trapezius thickness gains were superior. This directly contradicts a low per-session ceiling.','RCT','moderate','{"note": "~20 sets/session; 15-20 weekly sets; 10 weeks"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''Comparing Training Splits for Muscle Growth (20 STUDIES)'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Per-session ceiling - CONTRARY RCT (stacking was neutral)','Two further volume-matched trials found no penalty for stacking: 23 trained men (?3 yrs experience) doing 8 sets per exercise (up to 16 for leg curls) in one daily session 4x/week for 8 weeks matched an AM/PM split of the same volume; and 21 trained men (3.5 yrs) doing 2x/week at 16 sets/session matched 4x/week at 8 sets/session for lean mass of legs, arms and trunk over 9 weeks (caveat: that second study''s transcript does not confirm the 16 sets were all for one muscle).','RCT','moderate','{"note": "8 vs 16 sets/session, matched weekly; n=23 and n=21; 8-9 weeks"}'::jsonb,'Flow High Performance ''How Many Sets for Muscle Growth?'' and ''How Long Should Your Workouts Last?'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Per-session ceiling - retracted supporting evidence','The two 2020 Barbalho studies that had suggested a 5-10 weekly-set-per-session limit have been retracted because their reported data was established as atypical and improbable. They cannot be cited in support of any session cap, and their removal materially weakens the pre-existing evidence base for one.','retraction','strong','{"note": "originally 5/10/15/20 weekly sets at 1x/week"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''Comparing Training Splits for Muscle Growth (20 STUDIES)'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Per-session ceiling - acute-marker basis only','Every low per-session number outside the meta-analyses traces to acute protein-synthesis work, not measured growth: Ogasawara in rats (MPS rose to 10 sets but was lower at 20 sets, 3-min rest); Damas in humans (12 sets produced only slightly more MPS than 8, 2-min rest); and rodent data where MPS plateaued near 10 sets while protein breakdown kept rising, the sole mechanistic basis for claiming extra sets are net-negative rather than merely wasted.','mechanistic / acute markers','weak','{"note": "10 vs 20 sets (rats); 8 vs 12 sets (humans)"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''The ULTIMATE Guide to How Many Sets''; Menno Henselmans ''This is the max volume you should do per workout'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Per-session ceiling - sources'' own hedging','Presenters repeatedly and explicitly label the per-session ceiling as speculation: ''tentative,'' ''future research is unquestionably required,'' and one states outright that whether a per-session limit exists was a question current research had not answered as of that video, with updates planned when session-set research appears.','stated by sources','moderate',NULL,'House of Hypertrophy ''The ULTIMATE Guide'' / ''Comparing Training Splits'' / ''More Sets, More Growth'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Junk volume - does it reverse or only flatten','CONTESTED. One presenter concludes ''there doesn''t really seem to be such a thing as true junk volume,'' describing only diminishing returns and never reversal, citing a dose-response holding even at 45 weekly quad sets. The opposing position (rodent MPS/breakdown divergence, plus occasional trends toward detrimental effects that Henselmans notes appear ''almost exclusively'' at high volume per workout rather than high weekly volume) is real but thin and inconsistent. Notably, the only quantified caution about volume becoming counterproductive anywhere in the corpus concerns single-session concentration, and it is framed as occasional rather than established.','synthesis of conflicting sources','moderate',NULL,'Flow High Performance ''Is Junk Volume Real?''; Menno Henselmans ''Optimal volume & deloading'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Junk volume - the indirect, better-evidenced mechanism','Both channels agree that where junk volume is real, it operates indirectly: high set counts cause reduced rest, reduced effort/proximity to failure, degraded technique, or joint pain and irritation, any of which blunts the stimulus. The problem is therefore a session-quality problem, not a set-count problem, and is addressable by quality controls rather than by truncating volume.','reasoned practice, multi-source','moderate',NULL,'Flow High Performance ''Is Junk Volume Real?''; Jeff Nippard ''Junk Volume'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Junk volume - effort is the dominant real-world failure mode','In a cited study where lifters chose a self-estimated 10RM bench weight and went to true failure, only 22% got 10-12 reps, 31% got 13-15 (3-5 reps in reserve), and 47% got 16-20+ reps (6+ in reserve). Nippard calls this the most common form of junk volume - insufficient effort, not excess sets - which implies proximity-to-failure guidance buys more than a set cap does for the typical user.','study','moderate','{"note": "22% / 31% / 47%"}'::jsonb,'Jeff Nippard ''Junk Volume: Why You Should Avoid It For Max Muscle'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Frequency at equated weekly volume - the load-bearing null','Schoenfeld''s updated 2019 meta-analysis of 13 studies found no frequency or split superior when weekly variables are held constant; the 35-study meta and Nevis et al. 2022 independently found splitting a fixed weekly volume across different session counts produced similar growth. Crucially, 11 of Schoenfeld''s 13 studies used ?12 weekly sets per muscle, so this null is most defensible at or below 12 weekly sets.','meta-analysis','strong','{"note": "13 studies; 11 of 13 at ?12 weekly sets"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''Comparing Training Splits (20 STUDIES)''; Menno Henselmans 67-studies'),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Frequency - the small consistent positive for 2x/week','A meta-regression found distributing a muscle''s weekly volume across 2+ workouts marginally better than one weekly workout, with almost no further benefit from 3+. Once-weekly training was numerically lower in the 35-study data but the shortfall did not exceed typical error (average weekly volume in that data: 13 sets). The 67-study meta put only a 91% probability on frequency being positive for growth independent of volume - much weaker than its frequency-strength relationship. Net: 2+ sessions per muscle per week is never worse and possibly slightly better, at zero evidential cost.','meta-analysis','strong','{"note": "2+ sessions marginally better; 3+ adds ~nothing; 91% probability; 13 weekly sets average"}'::jsonb,'Flow High Performance ''How to Create a Hypertrophy Training Program'' / ''Which Training Split Should You Follow?''; House of Hypertrophy ''How Many Sets per Workout?''; Menno Henselmans 67-studies'),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Frequency - the mechanism is per-set quality','In volume-matched studies where total set count was identical, the higher-frequency group achieved greater total volume load (more weight or reps per set from being fresher) and saw slightly greater hypertrophy across most muscles. This identifies the real reason to spread volume: each set is performed better, not that frequency itself drives growth. It is also the strongest argument for redistributing a 17-set session rather than capping it.','study','strong',NULL,'Flow High Performance ''No. of Sets vs Volume Load'' / ''How Many Times Per Week Should a Muscle Be Trained?'' / ''High vs Low Frequency for Hypertrophy Training'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Frequency above 12 weekly sets - unresolved','Above 12 weekly sets per muscle, the five valid non-retracted split RCTs conflict without resolution: Evangelista and Bragato found no difference; Zaroni found full-body 5x superior to bro-split 1x; Bartolome found bro-split 1x superior to full-body 3-4x; Las Vegas found upper/lower 2x superior to full-body 3x. The narrator describes this literature as ''utterly confusing'' and offers no resolution.','conflicting RCTs','weak','{"note": "15-36 weekly sets; frequencies 1x-5x/week"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''Comparing Training Splits for Muscle Growth (20 STUDIES)'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Individual variation - the best-designed relevant study','Scarpelli et al. (2020), within-subject in 16 trained men over 8 weeks, raised one leg''s quad volume 20% above that person''s own baseline and assigned the other leg a fixed 22 sets/week (11 per session). The individualized +20% leg grew more, even though for 8 of the 16 subjects the fixed target represented a 30-120% increase over their baseline. Individualized examples: 5?6, 20?24, 40?48 weekly sets. Notably, quite a few subjects grew best doing far more than 6-8 sets per muscle per session.','within-subject RCT','strong','{"note": "+20% over baseline vs fixed 22 sets/week (11/session); n=16"}'::jsonb,'Stronger By Science ''Junk volume: Should you do a maximum number of sets per session?''; House of Hypertrophy ''Muscle Growth SLOWS DOWN Fast? (16 Studies)'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Individual variation - responder tiers scale with baseline delta','In post-hoc analyses of trained-subject volume trials, highest responders had increased weekly sets by an average of 6 above their own prior baseline, moderate responders by 4, and low responders by only 1-2 - suggesting the productive variable is the size of the increase relative to the individual''s own history, not any absolute target number.','study (post-hoc)','moderate','{"note": "+6 / +4 / +1-2 weekly sets vs own baseline"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''The ULTIMATE Guide'' / ''Muscle Growth SLOWS DOWN Fast? (16 Studies)''');
INSERT INTO public.corpus_findings
  (area, topic, claim, basis, confidence, quantities, source)
VALUES
  ('volume-per-muscle','Individual variation - unilateral split studies','Three unilateral designs quantify the spread: Damas (n=19, 6-9 vs 15 weekly quad sets) found 32% grew better on high, 32% on low, 36% similar; a Norwegian study (n=34, 6 vs 18 weekly sets) found 13 clearly favored 18 sets, 3 clearly favored 6, the rest similar; Van Vossel (n=21 untrained, 6-8 vs 9-12 weekly sets across four muscles) found 9-12 superior on average with most individuals matching the average. Direction of response is genuinely person-specific, but the population mean still favors more volume.','study','strong','{"note": "32/32/36%; 13 vs 3 of 34; 6-8 vs 9-12 weekly sets"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''How Many Sets Per Muscle? We Examine the Science'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Individual variation - magnitude range','Over 500 untrained subjects following an identical 12-week biceps curl protocol mostly gained 10-20% biceps cross-sectional area, with a small number gaining nothing and others gaining more than 50%. A separate review concludes true non-responders are unlikely to exist and low responders can improve by manipulating training variables.','study','strong','{"note": "n>500; 10-20% typical; 0% to >50% range"}'::jsonb,'Flow High Performance ''Are You Leaving Gains on the Table? (Yes)'' and ''How to Train Lagging Muscle Groups'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Individual variation - downside tail at extreme volume','A high-volume arm study using 28 weekly isolation sets (46 including compounds) with short rest produced highly variable outcomes including some subjects whose arm muscles DECREASED in size, potentially from excessive muscle damage. This is the strongest single piece of evidence in the corpus for guarding an upper bound, and it is a weekly-extreme, not a session-concentration, finding.','single study','moderate','{"note": "28 weekly isolation sets / 46 including compounds"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''How Many Sets Per Muscle? We Examine the Science'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Individual variation - expert consensus against any fixed number','Across four independent sources the same conclusion appears: no single optimal volume number exists. Nuckols states per-session and per-week tolerance ''varies more between individuals than a lot of people assume,'' framing 6-8 sets as a starting point for troubleshooting rather than a prescription; Henselmans says the optimal number ''doesn''t exist - it differs per individual,'' driven by recovery capacity, stress and sleep; Flow concludes volume cannot be quantified as a single number.','expert opinion, multi-source','moderate',NULL,'Stronger By Science; Menno Henselmans ''Optimal volume & deloading''; Flow High Performance ''Are You Leaving Gains on the Table?''; Renaissance Periodization'),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Set counting - how a generator should tally','The 35-study meta-analysis compared three counting methods - total (indirect set = full set), direct-only (indirect ignored), and fractional (indirect = half a set) - and found the fractional method fit the hypertrophy data best. A generator counting ''chest sets'' should therefore count direct chest work fully and indirect contribution at 0.5, which also materially changes what ''17 sets'' actually means.','meta-analysis (internal model comparison)','moderate','{"note": "indirect sets weighted 0.5"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''How Many Sets per Workout?'' / ''More Sets, More Growth'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Set counting - sets are an adequate unit','A systematic review concluded that when sets are taken close to failure in the 6-20 rep range, number of sets is an adequate way to quantify volume for hypertrophy; volume-load (sets x reps x load) is not required. Counting sets is defensible as the app''s volume unit, but only under the near-failure and rep-range precondition.','systematic review','strong','{"note": "6-20 rep range, close to failure"}'::jsonb,'Flow High Performance ''No. of Sets vs Volume Load'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Strength dosing is entirely different - do not share one number','The same datasets that model hypertrophy climbing to 43 weekly sets find strength essentially maximized at ~2 direct sets per muscle per session (3.5 counting fractional volume) and plateauing near 10 sets per week, with the caveat that median study length was only ~10 weeks, a period dominated by neural adaptation. Any volume cap must be goal-scoped; a hypertrophy number applied to a strength program is wrong by an order of magnitude.','meta-analysis','strong','{"note": "~2 direct sets/session, ~3.5 fractional; ~10 sets/week strength plateau vs 43 sets/week for size"}'::jsonb,'Menno Henselmans 67-studies; House of Hypertrophy ''How Many Sets per Workout?'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Muscle-specific - triceps is the only signal, and may be an artifact','Triceps is the only muscle where >20 weekly sets significantly beat 12-20. But study designs credit compound pressing toward triceps volume, so nominal prescriptions understate actual triceps exposure - Evangelista logged triceps at 40-48 indirect weekly sets versus biceps at 32 when both were nominally prescribed 16. The triceps ''needs more volume'' finding may therefore reflect set-counting convention rather than physiology, and should not be hard-coded as a per-muscle multiplier.','meta-analysis + study accounting','moderate','{"note": "triceps 40-48 vs biceps 32 indirect weekly sets at nominal 16"}'::jsonb,'Flow High Performance ''Is Junk Volume Real?''; House of Hypertrophy ''Comparing Training Splits (20 STUDIES)'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Muscle-specific - calves, the only direct small-muscle dose study','61 untrained women doing straight-leg calf raises 3x/week to failure at 15-20 reps for 6 weeks at 2, 3, or 4 sets/session (6, 9, or 12 weekly sets) grew most at 12 weekly sets - 73.9% more than the 6-set group and 17.6% more than the 9-set group on summed measures, though not all differences were statistically significant and the 6?9 gain exceeded the 9?12 gain. Critical confound: frequency was fixed at 3, so weekly sets and sets-per-session rose together; this cannot isolate a session effect.','study','moderate','{"note": "n=61; 6/9/12 weekly sets; +73.9% and +17.6%"}'::jsonb,'House of Hypertrophy ''Faster Muscle Growth: These NEW Studies Are Great'' / ''Bigger Calves: The ULTIMATE Guide (32 Studies)'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Muscle-specific - coach allocations are opinion, do not encode','Every per-muscle weekly allocation in the corpus is an illustrative coaching example, explicitly not evidence-derived, and the examples contradict each other: 12/12/12/12/8/8/8/8; 12/12/9/9/8/8/6; 18/14/15/8/15/8/8/8; 20 for biceps and calves vs 12 for chest/back/triceps/quads vs 4-8 for glutes/hams. Nippard''s claim that back, glutes and quads tolerate 10-12 sets/session versus 6-8 for other muscles is likewise uncited coaching experience. None of these should become a rule.','opinion','weak',NULL,'Flow High Performance ''How Much Volume for Each Muscle Group?'' / ''Volume Allocation'' / ''Part 4: Volume''; Jeff Nippard ''Junk Volume'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Small vs large muscle recovery - an evidence gap, not a finding','No source presents research differentiating recovery capacity between small and large muscles. What exists: a general statement that a muscle''s local recovery limit is ''generally not reached'' because systemic fatigue and joint tolerance give out first; an observation that calves show lower post-session fatigue, which the presenter cautions may be repeated-bout effect; and a fiber-type/frequency theory (slow-twitch tolerating higher frequency) that the presenter states is speculation, with the one relevant calf study finding no soleus/gastrocnemius difference. Israetel''s small-muscle claims (side delts resist damage, need more frequency) are uncited opinion and contradicted by another RP video putting delts among the highest-volume-needing muscles while chest needs little.','opinion / mechanistic','weak',NULL,'Flow High Performance ''Local & Systemic Recovery Capacity'' / ''How Many Times per Week''; House of Hypertrophy ''Bigger Calves''; Renaissance Periodization'),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Recovery is training-history dependent, not muscle-size dependent','Repeated-bout evidence: 10 untrained men doing 5x6 incline dumbbell curls had soreness peak ~48h and still above baseline at 96h on first exposure, but far milder and resolved by 96h after an identical session 7 days later; and in a 10-week eccentric leg-extension protocol, damage markers shrank from lasting 5+ days after session one to resolving within a day or two by the final week. Separately, 13 highly trained men performed full-body sessions at 0-1 RIR on four consecutive days with no notable performance decrement in bench or squat reps. Recovery windows should scale with a user''s training history, not with muscle size.','study','moderate','{"note": "soreness peak 48h, >96h vs <96h; 4 consecutive days at 0-1 RIR, n=13"}'::jsonb,'Flow High Performance ''Which Training Split Should You Follow?''; House of Hypertrophy ''More Sets, More Growth'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Exercise distribution within a muscle''s weekly volume','A systematic review found weekly sets per muscle to be the biggest predictor of hypertrophy even when frequency, load and rep range differ, from which the presenter extrapolates that splitting volume across more exercises versus more sets per exercise is unlikely to change growth - explicitly flagged as an extrapolation, since the review did not directly compare e.g. 4x3 versus 2x6 at 12 weekly sets. The practical recommendation is 2-4 exercises per muscle per week, with more variety for muscles of variable fiber orientation (pecs, lats, traps), itself presenter reasoning rather than evidence.','systematic review + explicit extrapolation','moderate','{"note": "2-4 exercises per muscle per week"}'::jsonb,'Flow High Performance ''Adding Exercises vs Adding Sets'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Exercise order','A meta-analysis found strength gains superior for exercises performed first in a session but hypertrophy gains similar regardless of order; the presenter notes no study has directly tested whether placing a priority muscle first produces superior growth for that specific muscle. Ordering a long session to front-load the priority muscle is defensible for effort quality but is not supported as a hypertrophy lever.','meta-analysis','moderate',NULL,'Flow High Performance ''How to Train Lagging Muscle Groups'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Within-session fatigue mechanism','The mechanistic case for a session cap: by roughly the sixth to tenth set for one muscle in a session, fatigue typically drops reps from ~10 to ~5 per set, which Henselmans argues roughly halves the per-set stimulus since accumulated mechanical tension is the primary hypertrophy driver. Flow makes the parallel ''volume efficiency'' argument from between-study data. Both are reasoning from fatigue, not from a study that measured set-by-set stimulus decline within a session.','mechanistic','weak','{"note": "reps ~10 ? ~5 by 6th-10th set"}'::jsonb,'Menno Henselmans ''This is the max volume you should do per workout''; Flow High Performance ''Complete Volume Guidelines'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Bro-split status - sources disagree with themselves','CONTESTED, including within single videos. Nippard says ~20 chest sets in one session leaves ''at least half of that volume pretty much junk'' yet in the same video states ''the latest research does show that bro splits still work just fine as long as weekly volume is matched,'' resolving only to practical efficiency rather than growth. Henselmans asserts bro-splits are suboptimal and that full-body is equal or better in ''every study he is aware of'' - an uncited sweeping claim contradicted by Bartolome. Flow calls a 16+ set chest day ''a difficult way to accumulate high volumes'' with quality ''probably quite low towards the end'' - an illustrative example, not a study finding.','opinion','weak','{"note": "16-20+ chest sets in one session"}'::jsonb,'Jeff Nippard ''Junk Volume''; Menno Henselmans ''max volume per workout''; Flow High Performance ''Training Splits for Muscle Growth'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Session-total budget (whole workout, not per muscle)','One coach''s practical guideline is roughly 15-25 total sets per workout across ALL muscles and exercises combined, typically landing at 3-4 sets per exercise. This is a whole-session budget and must not be conflated with a per-muscle ceiling - but it is the constraint a 17-set chest day most obviously violates, since 17 direct sets for one muscle nearly consumes an entire reasonable session.','practice','weak','{"note": "15-25 total sets per session; 3-4 sets per exercise"}'::jsonb,'Flow High Performance ''How to Create a Hypertrophy Training Program'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','The real binding constraints','Multiple sources converge that weekly volume is not limited by hypertrophy biology but by three practical ceilings - joint and connective-tissue stress, systemic fatigue, and time/willingness - with one stating time is ''the most likely factor to limit weekly volume in the majority of people,'' and another estimating that maximizing volume across all muscles would need ~12 hours a week. Flow''s own worked estimate (presenter opinion, not a study) puts a 15-set/week, 4-day, 60-minute routine at roughly 70-80% of the growth rate of a 30-set/week, 6-day, 2-hour routine - i.e. the marginal return on the top half of high volume is real but small relative to its time cost.','opinion / practice','moderate','{"note": "~12 hrs/week to maximize; 15 sets/wk ? 70-80% of 30 sets/wk growth rate"}'::jsonb,'Flow High Performance ''How Much Volume Should You Train Each Muscle With?'' / ''Are You Leaving Gains on the Table?''; Renaissance Periodization ''Not Growing? You Aren''t Training Enough'''),
  ('volume-per-muscle','Honest boundary - the decisive asymmetry','The corpus''s weekly-volume claims are anchored to named meta-analyses and controlled dose-response trials (12-20 band, 16/24/32, 22/42/52, 6/9/12 calves, 43-set curve). Its per-session claims rest on (a) one meta-analysis not controlled for weekly volume whose own number is an admitted statistical convention, (b) an unpublished analysis with a known trained/untrained bias, (c) two acute MPS studies including one in rats, and (d) two retracted papers. No study anywhere in the corpus holds weekly volume constant, varies only sets-per-session for one muscle, and measures growth. A per-session cap is the least evidenced construct in the entire area.','synthesis across all 8 batches','moderate',NULL,'Cross-batch synthesis: House of Hypertrophy, Flow High Performance, Menno Henselmans, Stronger By Science, Jeff Nippard, Renaissance Periodization'),
  ('female-cycle','cycle_phase - controlled evidence (the load-bearing negative result)','Colenso-Semple''s within-subject dissertation study (late-follicular peak-estrogen vs mid-luteal peak-progesterone, week-long integrated muscle protein synthesis via oral tracers plus unilateral exercise, 7 biopsies per participant, participants screened for 6+ months of tracked ovulatory cycles and no hormonal contraceptives for 1+ year) found NO difference in the muscle protein synthesis response to exercise between cycle phases, and she states the broader strength literature is now robust enough to say average strength performance does not differ by phase; she holds speed/power with less certainty because fewer studies exist. Barbell Medicine independently reports this same line of research as showing cycle phase does not meaningfully change how muscle responds to resistance training, noting the pushback comes specifically from influencers selling phase-specific protocols.','study','strong','{"note": "7 biopsies/participant; ages 18-30; 6+ months tracked ovulatory cycles; no OCP for 1+ year"}'::jsonb,'Andy Galpin channel - ''Female Training, Hormones & Nutrition: Fact vs. Fiction'' (Colenso-Semple); Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 2'),
  ('female-cycle','cycle_phase - the originator retracted the blanket rules','Dr. Stacy Sims, who popularized cycle-syncing training, has publicly revised her position, saying her earlier recommendations rested on science from ~5-6 years prior and explicitly cautioning against blanket rules such as ''train hard only in the low-hormone phase'' or ''deload for two weeks in the high-hormone phase''; she now recommends individuals track their own bleed pattern and subjective response instead of applying generic phase rules, and concedes the molecular-level findings should not be turned into training rules for ''the whole person.''','opinion','moderate',NULL,'Dr Stacy Sims - ''Should Women Train According to The Menstrual Cycle? What New Research Really Says'''),
  ('female-cycle','cycle_phase - live disagreement (keep both)','CONFLICT, unresolved in this corpus: Sims still argues from mechanism and clinical practice that the low-hormone follicular phase is the better window for PR attempts (better stress resilience, carbohydrate access, faster contraction, better sleep) while the luteal phase brings higher core temperature, reduced carbohydrate access, more amino-acid breakdown and higher RPE at the same absolute load; Colenso-Semple''s controlled data and her reading of the strength literature find no phase effect on protein synthesis or average strength. Notably, even a coach who endorses a soft cycle-syncing framework explicitly rejects the stricter version circulating online that reduces luteal-phase training to walking and yoga, arguing training only one week a month produces no meaningful adaptation. The app must not silently pick a side.','opinion','weak',NULL,'cross-source: Huberman Lab / Dr Stacy Sims channel vs Andy Galpin channel (Colenso-Semple); Eric Cressey - ''Julia Glanz Breaks Down Training Around the Menstrual Cycle'''),
  ('female-cycle','cycle_phase - phase cannot be reliably inferred from a calendar','Colenso-Semple states period-tracking apps that assume a fixed cycle-length-to-ovulation-timing relationship are making an assumption that is often false, citing two of her own study participants with identical 30-day cycles who ovulated consistently on day 9 and day 19; Sims separately states even women with regular, seemingly ovulatory cycles have roughly 4-5 anovulatory cycles per year, and that an anovulatory cycle produces a bleed with no luteal progesterone profile at all.','practice','moderate','{"note": "two 30-day cycles ovulating day 9 vs day 19; ~4-5 anovulatory cycles/year"}'::jsonb,'Andy Galpin channel (Colenso-Semple); Huberman Lab (Sims)'),
  ('female-cycle','cycle_phase - perimenopause degrades phase inference further','As women approach perimenopause an increasing share of cycles become anovulatory - a bleed occurs without ovulation and therefore without a progesterone-driven luteal phase - which Sims offers as one reason recovery and sleep decline in the mid-to-late 40s before other menopause symptoms appear. Practical consequence: any calendar-derived phase label becomes progressively less trustworthy exactly in the demographic most likely to opt into cycle features.','practice','moderate',NULL,'Dr Stacy Sims - ''Why Don''t I Recover Like I Used To?'''),
  ('female-cycle','cycle_phase - individual variation defeats the generic rule','Sims notes some women report feeling strongest in the few days before their period starts, so a generic instruction to deload during the luteal phase is actively wrong for them; her recommended substitute is tracking the individual''s own bleed pattern (flow intensity/timing, color, texture) and subjective response, since a change in typical bleed pattern or cycle length can itself signal reduced stress resilience worth flagging for training.','practice','moderate',NULL,'Dr Stacy Sims - ''Should Women Train According to The Menstrual Cycle?''');
INSERT INTO public.corpus_findings
  (area, topic, claim, basis, confidence, quantities, source)
VALUES
  ('female-cycle','cycle_phase - autoregulation heuristic (the only concrete in-app rule that survives)','Sims states heart rate variability is NOT a reliable indicator of what a woman''s body can handle on a given day (though an HRV drop does reasonably confirm ovulation occurred via the progesterone rise), and instead recommends a ''10-minute rule'': attempt the planned session for 10 minutes and, if the intended intensity still feels unattainable, scale down to recovery work rather than push through.','opinion','weak','{"note": "10 minutes"}'::jsonb,'Huberman Lab - ''Female-Specific Exercise & Nutrition for Health, Performance & Longevity'''),
  ('female-cycle','cycle_phase - nutrition adjustments (thin)','Sims recommends slightly increasing protein and carbohydrate around training in the luteal phase to offset progesterone-driven reductions in carbohydrate availability and increased amino-acid breakdown, but explicitly says the generic pattern should be overridden by the individual''s own tracked experience. A weightlifting coach separately describes eating 300-400 fewer calories with more carbohydrate in the follicular phase and adding two higher-calorie refeed days in the mid-to-late luteal phase (e.g. 1,700 ? 2,100 kcal), citing uncited research that women eat ~300 extra kcal/day in the luteal phase. Neither recommendation is backed by a controlled trial in this corpus.','opinion','weak','{"note": "follicular ?300-400 kcal; luteal refeed 1,700?2,100 kcal ? 2 days; claimed +~300 kcal/day luteal appetite (uncited)"}'::jsonb,'Dr Stacy Sims - ''What Every Woman Should Know About Your Menstrual Cycle''; Garage Strength - ''How To Cut Weight For Women'''),
  ('female-cycle','hormonal_contraception','No study has examined how oral contraceptive use affects long-term training adaptation in active women, per Sims, despite OCP users showing higher inflammatory and oxidative markers than naturally cycling women; she says progestin IUDs/implants are preferred by many tactical athletes for lacking systemic effects on adaptation, inflammation or mood. She frames a pill-induced bleed as a ''withdrawal bleed'' rather than a true period, meaning it carries no endocrine signal - so cycle tracking on a combined OCP has nothing physiological to track. Her further claims that OCP use thins emotional-processing cortex (unnamed ~2023 study, no authors or methodology given) and that a 2024 study found increased amygdala-linked fear responses and reduced risk-taking are uncorroborated and should not be surfaced in-app.','opinion','weak',NULL,'Huberman Lab (Sims); Dr Stacy Sims - ''What Every Woman Should Know About Your Menstrual Cycle'''),
  ('female-cycle','sex_differences - relative trainability is equal','A Bayesian meta-analysis found that at matched volume, hard-set count and proximity to failure, women gain the same relative percentage of muscle and the same relative strength as men; men''s larger absolute gains reflect a larger starting muscle mass (40 kg ? 10% = 4 kg vs 60 kg ? 10% = 6 kg). A separate 27-study meta-analysis reaches the same conclusion, despite women''s roughly 15-fold lower testosterone. Colenso-Semple and Galpin add that this relative-rate equivalence holds across the lifespan including through the peri/postmenopausal transition.','meta-analysis','strong','{"note": "27 studies in one meta-analysis; ~15x testosterone gap"}'::jsonb,'Biolayne - ''Women Don''t Need Special Resistance Training Programs''; Menno Henselmans; Andy Galpin channel'),
  ('female-cycle','sex_differences - within-session fatigue resistance','Women generally perform more repetitions than men at the same relative intensity (e.g. ~60% 1RM) and show a smaller drop in reps across sets within a session, consistent with the broader neuromuscular fatigue literature.','practice','moderate','{"note": "example ~60% 1RM"}'::jsonb,'Menno Henselmans - ''Do women recover faster than men?'''),
  ('female-cycle','sex_differences - between-session recovery is NOT established','The one cited recovery study (''sex differences may exist for performance fatigue but not recovery'', 4 sets to failure of curls and leg extensions retested at 4/24/48h) reported NO statistically significant sex difference in recovery: men''s curls ?7% at 4h, ?6% at 24h, ?0.7% at 48h vs women showing no drop at 4h and supercompensation at 24h; leg extension data were noisier (men ?3.4%/+3%/+2.7% vs women ?3.8%/+8%/?2.4%). The reviewer argues it was underpowered (SDs ~10 reps against differences of 1-2 reps) and that his read of the wider literature is women probably recover slightly faster - small enough that in practice he adds only a few extra sets per week, never extra recovery days or doubled volume.','study','weak','{"note": "curls: men ?7%/?6%/?0.7%; women ~0%/supercompensated; SD ~10 reps vs 1-2 rep effect"}'::jsonb,'Menno Henselmans - ''Do women recover faster than men?'''),
  ('female-cycle','sex_differences - coach claims to NOT build on','An Olympic weightlifting coach asserts, with no citations, that women have larger type 1 fibers making them more fatigue-resistant, prescribes sex-specific rest schemes (women ~1:00 after a triple / 1:10 after a double / 1:30 after a single vs men 1.5-2:00 after a triple), claims women recover better session-to-session (attributed to unpublished Larry Judge research with no sample or effect sizes) enabling up to 3 sessions/day for elites or 5-6 days/week recreationally, and claims estrogen is anti-catabolic and lets women handle more volume. None is corroborated; the matched-volume meta-analyses cut against building sex-differentiated rest or volume defaults.','opinion','weak','{"note": "women 1:00/1:10/1:30 vs men 1.5-2:00 rest; 3 sessions/day; 5-6 days/week"}'::jsonb,'Garage Strength - ''Olympic Weightlifting For Women'''),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - trainability is unimpaired (headline)','A 2026 meta-analysis pooling 126 training studies and roughly 4,000 women, comparing premenopausal to postmenopausal training outcomes, found essentially NO gap in strength gains, muscle gains, or fat loss, with age, training frequency and program duration also failing to predict any difference. Attribution is garbled across batches (rendered variously ''Eisenmann'', ''Izenman''), so cite the finding, not the author, until verified.','meta-analysis','strong','{"note": "126 studies, ~4,000 women"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 4 / ''Can You Still Build Muscle After Menopause?'''),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - trainability RCT','Eisenmann et al. 2023 (German Sport University Cologne) trained 41 women (mean age 52?4, classified pre/postmenopausal by FSH plus a 12-month period diary) on squat, bench and deadlift variants at ~75% 1RM twice weekly for 10 weeks. Premenopausal squat 1RM rose 48.5?68.5 kg and postmenopausal 36.3?71 kg; bench rose 18% (pre) vs 22% (post). CAUTION: the source reports these squat changes as ''61%'' and ''65%'' increases, which do not match the kilogram figures (68.5/48.5 ? 41%; 71/36.3 ? 96%) - an unresolved arithmetic inconsistency, so do not quote the percentages.','study','strong','{"note": "n=41, age 52?4, ~75% 1RM, 2x/wk, 10 wks; squat 48.5?68.5 kg vs 36.3?71 kg; bench +18% vs +22%"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 4 (Eisenmann 2023)'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - hypertrophy measurement artifact','In the same Eisenmann 2023 trial, direct ultrasound quadriceps thickness (rectus femoris, vastus lateralis) increased in BOTH pre- and postmenopausal groups with no statistically significant between-group difference, while bioelectrical-impedance-derived fat-free mass showed a small gain only in the premenopausal group and no change or slight loss in the postmenopausal group - attributed to BIA being too imprecise to detect a lean-mass change of ~1.5 kg or less. Practical: BIA-based progress readouts will manufacture false ''menopause blunts gains'' narratives.','study','moderate','{"note": "~1.5 kg BIA detection floor"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 4'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - trainability at extreme age','Fiatarone''s 1990 JAMA trial put ~9-10 nursing-home residents averaging age 90 through 8 weeks of progressive resistance training, after which strength rose ~174% and walking speed improved ~48%, with muscle size increasing and some participants abandoning canes; her 1994 follow-up in over 100 people (63 of them women in their 80s-90s) replicated the strength and muscle gains with no discernible menopause-related blunting.','study','strong','{"note": "1990: n?9-10, age ~90, 8 wks, strength +174%, gait speed +48%; 1994: n>100 incl. 63 women"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Parts 2 and 4'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - ''no special program'' (contested position)','Barbell Medicine''s stated position is that ''the training that works for a perimenopausal woman is the training that works for any healthy adult'' and that no menopause-specific training protocol is supported by evidence, explicitly rejecting menopause-specific certifications, proprietary programs and supplement stacks as marketing. This is a normative stance from one source group; the corpus contains no equally-evidenced dissent, but Sims and the commercial menopause-fitness sector disagree.','opinion','moderate',NULL,'Barbell Medicine - multiple episodes'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - the concrete baseline prescription','The evidence-based prescription given for a peri/postmenopausal woman is identical to that for any healthy adult: lift 2+ days/week covering all major muscle groups with mostly compound movements at roughly 60-80% 1RM for sets of 3-12 reps with a couple of reps in reserve; 150-300 min/week moderate conditioning (able to speak short sentences, not sing) or ~75-150 min vigorous when time-constrained; ~1.6 g/kg/day protein with 1.2 g/kg described as also fine while training; progressively add load over years. Two 30-45 minute lifting sessions per week plus a little cardio is described as covering most of the benefit for time-constrained women.','practice','moderate','{"note": "2+ d/wk, 60-80% 1RM, 3-12 reps, RIR ~2; 150-300 min moderate or 75-150 min vigorous; 1.6 g/kg (1.2 g/kg floor); 30-45 min sessions"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 4 / ''The Three Fears That Keep Women Out of the Gym'''),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - frequency vs volume (contested interpretation)','Orsatti 2022, 113 postmenopausal women in a 16-week program, found twice-weekly training had a higher non-response rate than three-times-weekly. The source disputes the ''must train 3x/week'' reading, arguing the driver was total volume/load and frequency is only a distribution tool. Same trial: each 1-unit increase in HOMA-IR was associated with ~40% higher odds of non-response (HOMA-IR 1 vs 3 ? doubled risk), a pattern the source says is not menopause-specific and also appears in older adults, type 2 diabetes and obesity. Separately, the Eisenmann 2023 authors speculated postmenopausal women may need MORE weekly sets for equal hypertrophy; Barbell Medicine calls this an open question the trial does not answer - insufficient to justify a menopause-triggered volume bump in a program generator.','study','moderate','{"note": "n=113, 16 wks, 2x vs 3x/wk; ~40% higher non-response odds per HOMA-IR unit"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 4 (Orsatti 2022; Eisenmann 2023)'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - mechanism (why symptoms, not estrogen per se, may matter)','CONFLICT on mechanism. Barbell Medicine argues menopausal symptoms (sleep disruption, low energy, reduced motivation, altered appetite) indirectly impair training response rather than declining estrogen directly blunting muscle''s response, analogising to men with clinically low testosterone who still gain strength and muscle, just less. A competing account (also Barbell Medicine, estrogen episode) describes estrogen as an anti-catabolic ''shield'' preserving satellite cell function, whose withdrawal raises apoptosis and makes muscle QUALITY (force per unit mass) decline faster than muscle size, with measurable drops in jump power and grip strength even when lean mass barely moves. Sims goes further with an uncorroborated ''myosin dysfunction'' account (estrogen governing myosin-actin binding), in direct tension with Colenso-Semple''s finding that nothing measurably happens to strength as a direct result of menopause.','mechanistic','weak',NULL,'Barbell Medicine - ''The Estrogen Shield'' and Part 4; Dr Stacy Sims channel; Andy Galpin channel'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - bone loss natural history (timing is the actionable part)','The SWAN bone substudy (~2,000 women, serial DEXA across the final menstrual period, 2012) found bone loss is NOT linear: slow before the transition, accelerating entering perimenopause, peaking in the 1-2 years around the final menstrual period, then slowing within a few years. Peak annual loss ~1.8%/yr lumbar spine and ~1.0-1.2%/yr femoral neck; cumulative loss across the ~5-year transmenopausal window ~10.6% lumbar and ~9% femoral neck. CONFLICT: Sims states women lose 15-20% of bone density during perimenopause (and elsewhere ''about one-third of bone mass'') - treat magnitude as unsettled and prefer the SWAN figures.','study','strong','{"note": "n?2,000; peak 1.8%/yr lumbar, 1.0-1.2%/yr femoral neck; cumulative ~10.6% / ~9% over ~5 yrs; Sims claims 15-20% (also ''one-third'')"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 3 (Greendale/SWAN 2012) vs Dr Stacy Sims channel'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - heavy loading builds bone (strongest programming-relevant RCT)','LIFTMOR (Beck''s group, JBMR 2018): 101 postmenopausal women, mean age 65, all with T-scores below ?1 at lumbar spine or femoral neck, did 8 months of twice-weekly 30-minute supervised sessions of 5?5 above 80-85% 1RM on deadlift, back squat and overhead press plus jumping chin-ups with straight-leg drop landings, versus a home band/bodyweight control. Lumbar spine BMD rose 2.9% vs ?1.2% in controls; femoral neck rose 0.3%/held steady vs ?1.9%; both significant; 92% compliance; one mild fully-resolved back strain across 2,613 supervised sessions and zero fractures. CAVEAT: the same podcast elsewhere cites ''~4% lumbar / ~2% hip'' for this trial, so verify against Watson et al. 2018 before fixing a number in product copy; also an 8-month surrogate endpoint under expert supervision, with relative intensity likely drifting down as subjects got stronger without 1RM retesting.','study','strong','{"note": "n=101, age 65, 8 mo, 2x/wk, 5?5 @80-85% 1RM; lumbar +2.9% vs ?1.2%; femoral neck +0.3% vs ?1.9%; 92% compliance; 1 injury / 2,613 sessions"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Parts 3 and 4; ''Lifting Heavy During and After Menopause'''),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - bone replication and long-term','MedX-OP randomized 115 postmenopausal women with low bone mass to 8 months of heavy resistance training vs a Pilates-based bone program: lifting gained 1.9% lumbar spine BMD vs 0.1% for Pilates (within error), and in the subgroup not on bone medication Pilates participants actually LOST lumbar BMD while lifting still gained 1.9%; lifting also beat Pilates on back-extensor strength, leg strength and five-times sit-to-stand, with equal tolerability and no worsening of pelvic floor disability scores. EFOPS, the longest controlled trial in this space at 16 years in early postmenopausal women, found lumbar spine BMD dropped 4.5% in non-exercising controls vs 1.2% (essentially preserved) in a multicomponent exercise group including resistance training, with no major adverse events attributed to exercise over 16 years.','study','strong','{"note": "MedX-OP n=115, 8 mo, +1.9% vs +0.1%; EFOPS 16 yrs, ?4.5% control vs ?1.2% exercise"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 3 / ''Lifting Heavy During and After Menopause'''),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - bone loading intensity threshold (CONFLICT, matters for the generator)','CONFLICT with direct product consequences. Sims (as characterized by Barbell Medicine) claims only loads above ~85% 1RM meaningfully benefit bone and that perimenopause is a closing window. Barbell Medicine disputes both, arguing that when only genuinely heavy-loading studies are examined, bone density benefits appear across roughly 65-85% 1RM a couple of days per week, that bone responds across a load range once training is heavy enough, and that women are ''basically never locked out'' of building bone later in life. Both agree on the negative: meta-analyses showing weak bone effects pool underdosed interventions (light machines, bands, small dumbbells, water-based training - water aerobics specifically delivering essentially no skeletal loading).','opinion','moderate','{"note": "disputed threshold 65-85% 1RM vs >85% 1RM"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 4 / ''Lift Heavy or Lose Your Bones?'' vs Dr Stacy Sims'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - do not soften programming for low bone density','Barbell Medicine''s clinical position: a postmenopausal woman with osteopenia (T-score ?1 to ?2.5; worked example ?1.4 lumbar spine / ?1.6 femoral neck) should NOT be started more conservatively or steered away from deadlifting relative to a matched person without osteopenia - dose by current fitness level and progress as tolerated rather than gating on the T-score. This reverses a 1995 ACSM recommendation telling osteopenic/osteoporotic patients to avoid spinal loading. Exception: a prior fragility fracture automatically upgrades classification to osteoporosis regardless of T-score.','practice','moderate','{"note": "osteopenia T-score ?1 to ?2.5; example ?1.4 / ?1.6"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 3 / ''Osteopenia? Lift Heavy'''),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - bone density is the wrong endpoint to promise','Two limits on the bone story: (1) Siris et al. 2001 found 82% of postmenopausal women presenting with a fragility fracture had bone density that would NOT have qualified them for an osteoporosis diagnosis on DEXA, so most fractures occur in the osteopenic-or-normal range; (2) the training trials measured DEXA BMD, not fractures, so ''heavy lifting prevents fractures'' is plausible but not directly tested - the source states this caveat itself. Falls are the more directly modifiable path: a 2019 Cochrane review of 108 trials and >23,000 community-dwelling older adults found exercise reduced fall RATE by about 23%, strongest in programs combining balance training with progressive resistance training.','study','strong','{"note": "82% of fracture patients below the osteoporosis threshold; Cochrane 108 trials, >23,000 adults, ~23% fall-rate reduction"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 3 / ''Lifting Heavy During and After Menopause'''),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - modality ranking','Pilates, yoga, tai chi and water aerobics are described as insufficient on their own to meet muscle- and bone-strengthening guidelines, with water aerobics singled out as delivering essentially no skeletal loading; Pilates/yoga are framed as acceptable low-barrier entry activities for previously inactive people provided heavier loading is added later. Sims independently agrees Pilates and yoga are valuable for isometrics, proprioception, balance and meditation but ''do not move the needle'' on pure strength, fast-twitch function, or bone density.','opinion','moderate',NULL,'Barbell Medicine - ''Lifting Heavy During and After Menopause''; Dr Stacy Sims channel'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - body composition, honest magnitude','SWAN body composition (Greendale et al. 2019; ~3,000 women, 7 US sites, 10+ years serial DEXA): the rate of fat gain roughly DOUBLED during the transition, from ~0.25 kg/yr before to ~0.5 kg/yr during, lasting about 3.5 years around the final menstrual period - totalling about 1.5 kg of fat gain and about 200 g of lean mass loss attributable to menopause itself, against a typical US midlife total gain of 10-15 kg between ages 30 and 60. Menopause explains ~1.5 kg of that; the remaining 8-13 kg is aging, sleep, activity and diet that predate and outlast the transition.','study','strong','{"note": "~3,000 women, 10+ yrs; 0.25?0.5 kg/yr for ~3.5 yrs; ~1.5 kg fat + ~200 g lean attributable; vs 10-15 kg total midlife gain"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 3 / ''Does Menopause Really Cause Weight Gain?'''),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - visceral fat redistribution is the real signal','Lovejoy et al. (Pennington Biomedical): 156 women in their 40s-50s followed 4 years with serial DEXA and abdominal CT; only the 38 women who actually transitioned to postmenopause showed a significant visceral fat rise, on the order of 40% around the final menstrual period, while women who stayed premenopausal showed no comparable increase (transitioner subgroup explicitly noted as modest, n=38). The redistribution phenomenon is called ''uncontested'' and attributed to estrogen loss plus muscle loss plus any positive energy balance - explicitly NOT to elevated cortisol, disputing the ''cortisol belly'' explanation. SWAN also shows a paradoxical association where HIGHER bioavailable testosterone across the transition may contribute to central fat accumulation.','study','moderate','{"note": "n=156, 4 yrs, 38 transitioners, visceral fat ~+40%"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 3'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - metabolism does not crash','Pontzer et al., Science 2021, using doubly-labeled water in over 6,400 adults across 29 countries and adjusting for body size, found total energy expenditure stays relatively constant from about age 20 to 60 with NO menopause-specific dip, declining only ~0.7%/yr after age 60; a 2023 follow-up (Karppinen et al.) confirmed the same pattern for resting metabolic rate specifically in midlife women. Directly contradicts the ''menopause crashes your metabolism'' claim.','study','strong','{"note": ">6,400 adults, 29 countries; flat ages 20-60; ?0.7%/yr after 60"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 3 / ''Does Menopause Really Cause Weight Gain?''');
INSERT INTO public.corpus_findings
  (area, topic, claim, basis, confidence, quantities, source)
VALUES
  ('female-cycle','menopause - cardiometabolic changes attributable to the transition','Matthews et al., JACC 2009, SWAN data on just over 1,000 women: in the two years around the final menstrual period LDL rose ~5 mg/dL/yr and apolipoprotein B ~3 mg/dL/yr, totalling a menopause-attributable rise of ~10 points LDL and ~6 points apoB beyond aging - characterized by the AHA scientific statement as real but modest. Janssen 2008 (SWAN) found late-peri/early-postmenopausal women had 45-60% higher odds of metabolic syndrome than same-age premenopausal women, but adjusting for visceral fat redistribution removed most of the effect, placing visceral fat as the proximate driver and the hormonal change upstream.','study','moderate','{"note": "n>1,000; LDL +5 mg/dL/yr, apoB +3 mg/dL/yr (? +10 / +6 total); metabolic syndrome odds +45-60%, mostly explained by visceral fat"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 3'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - MHT: what it does and does not do','A 2021 RCT had postmenopausal women do 12 weeks of resistance training with transdermal estradiol or placebo: placebo gained ~4% quadriceps cross-sectional area, estradiol ~8% - estrogen roughly doubled the hypertrophy response, but training produced substantial growth either way. A meta-analysis of 23 studies and ~9,000 women found the average MHT strength advantage is about 5%, real but modest and downstream of the training. Estrogen replacement studies show no difference in LEAN MASS between users and non-users, and SWAN shows lean mass fairly stable across the transition. Scope: MHT is strongly effective for vasomotor and genitourinary symptoms (local vaginal estrogen) but NOT well-supported for body composition, lipid lowering, cognitive protection, or established osteoporosis with prior fragility fracture (dedicated pharmacotherapy preferred); its main weight-relevant effect is indirect, via better sleep and adherence. Premature ovarian insufficiency (before age 40) requires a distinct higher-dose protocol until the average natural menopause age.','study','strong','{"note": "12 wks, +4% vs +8% quad CSA; 23 studies, ~9,000 women, +5% strength; POI defined as onset before age 40"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - ''The Estrogen Shield'' / Menopause Parts 3 and 4; Andy Galpin channel'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - MHT does not prevent dementia','Three major RCTs converge on no cognitive protection: WHIMS (~4,500 women aged 65+) found the combined estrogen+progestin arm had roughly DOUBLE the risk of probable dementia vs placebo, about 23 extra cases per 10,000 women per year; KEEPS-Cog (2015, ~700 women aged 42-58 within 3 years of final menstrual period, 4 years of estrogen) had a null primary cognitive endpoint with some secondary mood benefit in the oral arm; ELITE (2016, 600+ women, early [<6 yrs post-menopause] vs late [10+ yrs] estradiol initiation) found no clinically significant cognitive effect in either group. The Lancet Commission''s 12 modifiable dementia risk factors do not include menopause. Trials were not powered to rule out smaller effects.','study','strong','{"note": "WHIMS ~4,500, ~2x dementia risk, +23 cases/10,000/yr; KEEPS-Cog ~700, null; ELITE 600+, null"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - ''Does Hormone Therapy Prevent Dementia?'' / Menopause Part 3'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - cognition and mood windows (real, time-boxed)','The SWAN cognition substudy (~2,300 women, serial neuropsychological testing, observational) found processing speed dropped somewhat more than aging alone predicts during LATE perimenopause (small-to-moderate effect) with a smaller drop in memory, and both returned to the normal age-related trajectory once women reached early postmenopause - a transient dip, not a permanent deficit. Roughly half to two-thirds of perimenopausal women report cognitive complaints (''brain fog''), the second most common complaint after vasomotor symptoms. Separately, the Harvard Study of Moods and Cycles, the SWAN mental health study and others converge on a 2-to-4-fold increase in odds of NEW-ONSET major depression in late perimenopause vs premenopause, tracking the hormonal instability of late perimenopause specifically rather than the more stable postmenopausal state.','study','strong','{"note": "n?2,300; ~50-67% report brain fog; 2-4x new-onset major depression odds in late perimenopause"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 3 / ''Does Hormone Therapy Prevent Dementia?'''),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - exercise is not a hot flash treatment','The North American Menopause Society''s 2023 non-hormone-therapy position statement does not recommend exercise as a primary treatment for vasomotor symptoms, and a 2022 systematic review found exercise reduced hot flash SEVERITY slightly but did not change how OFTEN hot flashes occurred. Do not market training as a hot flash fix.','meta-analysis','strong',NULL,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 4'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - supplements: what to say','Testosterone: a meta-analysis of 36 trials and >8,000 women, plus a concurring 2026 review, found NO real benefit of exogenous testosterone in women for body composition, muscle or bone; the only approved indication in women is hypoactive sexual desire disorder, with body-composition effects only at doses pushing levels above the normal female range. Protein: a 2026 review pooling 34 strength-training studies in ~1,500 postmenopausal women found that in the 9 studies isolating protein intake, adding protein above a low floor (~1.2-1.6 g/kg/day) added nothing to strength or muscle beyond training itself. Creatine: a Dos Santos meta-analysis of 10 RCTs in older women found small-to-moderate strength benefits, larger in trials over 24 weeks - the same magnitude seen outside this population, not a uniquely large effect; 3-5 g/day or 0.03-0.05 g/kg/day, no loading phase, no increase in total body water outside loading (reported bloating attributed to ~20 g/day loading doses). Vitamin D: below ~20 nmol/L clearly warrants supplementation (less certainty for a 30 nmol/L cutoff), no evidence for pushing beyond sufficiency. Calcium: a Kim meta-analysis found ~15% higher cardiovascular disease risk (concentrated in coronary heart disease) with supplemental calcium in postmenopausal women - explicitly labeled CONTESTED, not settled.','meta-analysis','strong','{"note": "T: 36 trials, >8,000 women; protein: 34 studies, ~1,500 women, 9 isolating protein, 1.2-1.6 g/kg; creatine: 10 RCTs, 3-5 g/day; vit D <20 nmol/L; calcium ~15% CVD risk (contested)"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 4'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - anabolic resistance is conditional, not automatic','A study of 215 healthy non-obese adults across a wide age range found NO difference between young and old, or between men and women, in basal muscle protein synthesis. The ''anabolic resistance'' literature (needing ~25-40 g protein per meal in older adults vs ~20 g in younger) is concentrated in inactive/sedentary populations, especially with metabolic disease or bed rest, and even a short bout of activity such as a 10-minute walk before a meal is reported to largely eliminate it. CONFLICT: Sims asserts aging women are anabolically resistant and need higher per-dose protein at regular intervals (arguing the RDA derives from sedentary older men), and separately claims 35 g leucine-rich protein within 45 minutes post-training for reproductive-age women rising to 40-60 g for perimenopausal-and-older, with women returning to metabolic baseline within ~60 minutes vs up to 3 hours for men - none of these Sims figures are corroborated anywhere else in this corpus, and one of her own transcripts gives no gram target at all.','study','moderate','{"note": "n=215; ~20 g (young) vs 25-40 g (older) per meal; Sims uncorroborated: 35 g / 40-60 g within 45 min, 60 min vs 3 h"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 4 vs Huberman Lab / Dr Stacy Sims channel'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - mortality levers (what to actually optimize)','US vital statistics show osteoporosis is not among the top five causes of death for women in any age band from 45 onward and is the underlying cause in under 1% of deaths at every age. The largest modifiable levers: a 2009 meta-analysis of 33 studies and >100,000 people found each 1-MET increase in cardiorespiratory fitness associated with ~13% lower all-cause mortality (far more conservative than the widely-cited ~80% figure from the 2018 JAMA Cleveland Clinic cohort, which compared only top vs bottom deciles of a clinically-referred population); a 2022 BJSM meta-analysis of 16 studies found resistance training associated with ~15% lower all-cause mortality, rising to ~40% combined with aerobic training; a 2025 Brazilian cohort (~3,900 adults aged 46-75, median 11-year follow-up) found the lowest muscle-POWER group had roughly 6-fold higher mortality risk than the highest, with grip strength a much weaker predictor. PURE (~140,000 people, 17 countries) found ~17% higher mortality per 5 kg lower grip - but grip is a marker of overall neuromuscular quality, and training grip directly raises the score without the mortality benefit.','meta-analysis','strong','{"note": "osteoporosis <1% of deaths; 13% lower mortality per +1 MET (33 studies, >100k); RT ~15% / RT+aerobic ~40% (16 studies); ~6x mortality gradient by power (n?3,900); PURE 17% per ?5 kg grip"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 3'),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - the actual baseline problem is non-participation','Accelerometer-measured data show fewer than 5% of US adults over 40 meet the 150 min/week moderate-to-vigorous guideline - about 3.4% of women aged 40-49 and about 2.7% of women aged 50-59 - with average sedentary time over 7 hours/day, versus 42% of women who SELF-REPORT meeting it and only about 15% who report lifting weights at all. Barbell Medicine adds that primary care doctors counsel patients on exercise only about 1 in 10 visits (uncited), and suspect a specific heavy-lifting prescription is rarer still.','study','strong','{"note": "<5% overall; 3.4% (40-49), 2.7% (50-59) objective vs 42% self-reported; 15% report lifting; >7 h/day sedentary"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 3 / ''Why Women Should Lift Heavy'''),
  ('female-cycle','menopause - decline rates and what training preserves','The 2005 Baltimore Longitudinal Study of Aging (375 women, median 8-year VO2max follow-up) found aerobic decline accelerates each decade after 30 - ~8-10% per decade in the 40s, 10-14% per decade in the 50s - with the menopause-specific piece estimated at only about 1% per decade. A whole-body MRI study of 468 women found skeletal muscle mass holds steady until about age 50 then declines ~0.7%/yr while STRENGTH declines roughly three times faster. In explicitly selection-biased comparisons, lifelong-trained women in their 60s showed VO2max of 45-55 vs 22-28 in sedentary age-matched controls, and a 2024 longitudinal analysis of competitive powerlifters found women over 59 who kept competing gained about 2-5% strength per year versus the general population losing about 1% per year - findings the sources caveat as showing what is biologically possible for committed exercisers, not what a previously-sedentary woman starting at 55 will achieve.','study','strong','{"note": "VO2max ?8-10%/decade (40s), ?10-14%/decade (50s), menopause-specific ~1%/decade; muscle ?0.7%/yr after 50, strength ~3x faster; trained 60s VO2max 45-55 vs 22-28; powerlifters 59+ +2-5%/yr vs ?1%/yr"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Parts 2 and 3'),
  ('female-cycle','pelvic_floor - heavy lifting does not appear to damage it (contested)','A 2024 randomized crossover study of 47 strength-trained women performing 4?4 at 75-85% 1RM on back squat and deadlift, each woman her own control, found NO differences in pelvic floor resting pressure, contraction strength or endurance versus a rest condition; a separate trial randomizing inactive, overweight and obese women to 12 weeks of strength training vs inactive controls found no difference in new-onset urinary incontinence. Cross-sectional studies do find pelvic floor symptoms in 30-40% of some samples of women who lift heavy, which the source argues reflects general prevalence (especially post-childbirth) rather than causation, since matched non-lifting groups show similar rates - with the honest analogy that exertion-triggered symptoms look artificially rare in people who never exert. Supporting: a 2021 survey of competitive women powerlifters (Wakander et al.) found incontinence associated with AGE and NUMBER OF CHILDREN but only weakly and non-significantly with years of powerlifting. STILL CONTESTED: some pelvic health specialists hold that belted heavy lifting with Valsalva does raise intra-abdominal pressure and incontinence risk; the source rates itself only ''moderately confident'' pending a direct before/after study.','study','moderate','{"note": "n=47, 4?4 @75-85% 1RM; 12-week trial; 30-40% symptom prevalence cross-sectionally"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - ''Is Heavy Lifting Safe With Low Bone Density?'' / Menopause Part 4; Stronger By Science (Wakander et al. 2021)'),
  ('female-cycle','pelvic_floor - treatment works, and is the actionable intervention','A 2018 Cochrane review of 31 trials in over 1,800 women found women with stress urinary incontinence were roughly EIGHT TIMES more likely to report a cure with pelvic floor muscle training than without; a 2024 study in CrossFit women with stress urinary incontinence found 16 weeks of home-based pelvic floor muscle training produced symptom improvement in 64% of the intervention group vs 8% of controls.','meta-analysis','strong','{"note": "Cochrane: 31 trials, >1,800 women, ~8x cure odds; 2024: 16 wks, 64% vs 8%"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 4'),
  ('female-cycle','pelvic_floor - referral boundary (buildable rule)','Barbell Medicine''s referral guidance: transient leaking or heaviness during a lifting set is not itself a reason to stop and probably will not worsen the underlying issue, but PERSISTENT leaking should prompt referral to a pelvic floor physical therapist, with OBGYN referral reasonable for additional or concerning symptoms.','practice','moderate',NULL,'Barbell Medicine - ''Is Heavy Lifting Safe With Low Bone Density?'' / ''The Three Fears That Keep Women Out of the Gym'''),
  ('female-cycle','pregnancy - benefits (the case for continuing to train)','A meta-analysis of 50 controlled studies of resistance training during pregnancy found it lowered the odds of C-section by 16%, gestational hypertension by 58%, gestational diabetes by 38%, perinatal mood disorders by 52%, and macrosomia by 33%, describing strength training as very safe with no adverse effects on any health or birth outcome; a separate systematic review found reduced excessive weight gain, alleviated pain, improved mood and better health-related quality of life. Maternal exercise is also associated with roughly 200-400 g lower average birth weight, framed as favorable (easier labor) rather than harmful.','meta-analysis','strong','{"note": "50 studies; C-section ?16%, gestational hypertension ?58%, gestational diabetes ?38%, perinatal mood disorders ?52%, macrosomia ?33%; birth weight ?200-400 g"}'::jsonb,'Menno Henselmans - ''Lifting Weights While Pregnant''; Stronger By Science podcast'),
  ('female-cycle','pregnancy - heavy load safety data','A controlled study had pregnant women perform squat, bench press and deadlift at 70%, 80% and 90% of 10RM including with the Valsalva maneuver, and found no negative effect on fetal health markers (umbilical blood flow metrics, fetal heart rate) even at the heaviest loads. A cited study of Olympic weightlifters and powerlifters found typical perinatal and pelvic-floor outcomes regardless of Olympic lifts, Valsalva use, or supine lifting. Traditional advice against heavy lifting partly derives from epidemiology linking repeated heavy OCCUPATIONAL lifting to birth complications, confounded by other job stress; research comparing exercising to non-exercising pregnant women finds fewer complications among exercisers.','study','moderate','{"note": "70/80/90% of 10RM"}'::jsonb,'Biolayne - ''Pregnant Women Shouldn''t Train Hard is BS''; Menno Henselmans'),
  ('female-cycle','pregnancy - largest observational cohort of heavy lifters','Previtt et al. 2026, an observational survey of 679 self-selected lifters (mostly Olympic weightlifters and CrossFitters) who trained above 80% 1RM during pregnancy - 85% continued heavy resistance training, 72% continued weightlifting-style training, 71% did supine exercise, 34% used Valsalva, 24% maintained pre-pregnancy training to delivery - found gestational hypertension ~3% (vs ~7% general population), gestational diabetes ~1% (vs ~8%), and postpartum depression/anxiety ~7% (vs ~17%), with Olympic lifting, Valsalva and supine training not associated with meaningfully higher complication rates within the sample. Women who maintained pre-pregnancy training to delivery had ~50% lower complication rates (OR 0.49) - but the hosts flag three layers of self-selection bias and note reverse causation is equally plausible (women who developed complications may be the ones who scaled back). Postpartum urinary incontinence appeared in 57% of this sample vs ~33% in the general population but was not predicted by any exercise variable within it.','study','moderate','{"note": "n=679; GH 3% vs 7%, GD 1% vs 8%, PPD/anxiety 7% vs 17%; OR 0.49; postpartum incontinence 57% vs 33%"}'::jsonb,'Stronger By Science podcast, citing Previtt et al. 2026'),
  ('female-cycle','pregnancy - guideline history and why the remaining cautions exist','ACOG''s 1985 guidance capped heart rate at 140 bpm and sessions at 15 minutes and advised previously inactive women not to start, withdrawn around 1994 and described by multiple sources as ''based on vibes''; 2002 recommended 30 min moderate most days; 2008 first permitted vigorous intensity (?150 min/week, continuation not initiation); the current 2020 guidelines, reaffirmed 2026, treat most exercise as acceptable with cautions against scuba diving, altitude for sea-level residents, exercise in heat (first-trimester overheating linked to neural tube defect risk), very heavy resistance training, supine exercise, and Valsalva. Critically, those last three cautions are explicitly NOT based on demonstrated harm but on mechanistic plausibility: heavy lifting and Valsalva raise DIASTOLIC blood pressure (unlike aerobic exercise, which raises systolic while diastolic stays flat), creating whole-cardiac-cycle pressure of unestablished fetal safety; relaxin loosens tendons and ligaments in T2/T3 raising injury risk under load; supine risk relates to fetal weight compressing the inferior vena cava and aorta.','practice','moderate','{"note": "1985: HR<140 bpm, 15 min; 2002: 30 min most days; 2008: ?150 min/wk; 2020 (reaffirmed 2026)"}'::jsonb,'Stronger By Science podcast; Barbell Medicine; Huberman Lab (Sims)'),
  ('female-cycle','pregnancy - concrete intensity prescription (buildable)','Girls Gone Strong (Molly Galbraith) gives a maximum pregnancy intensity of about 8.5/10 perceived effort for a woman who exercised vigorously pre-pregnancy vs about 7/10 for one who did not; a woman STARTING a new program during pregnancy (encouraged by all major governing bodies once medically cleared with no contraindications, contrary to folk belief) begins at ~3/10 and progresses only to ~6/10. RPE is preferred over a heart-rate cap for the general population because perceived exertion and heart rate can diverge by as much as 42 bpm; elite/highly-trained pregnant athletes are instead advised to use a monitor and stay below 85-90% of max HR because they underestimate their own exertion.','practice','moderate','{"note": "8.5/10 (prior vigorous) vs 7/10; new starters 3/10 ? 6/10; up to 42 bpm RPE-HR divergence; elites <85-90% HRmax"}'::jsonb,'Eric Cressey Physical Preparation Podcast - Molly Galbraith'),
  ('female-cycle','pregnancy - rep range CONFLICT','CONFLICT. Major governing bodies recommend pregnant lifters stay in a 12-15 rep range; Girls Gone Strong disputes this for advanced/experienced lifters, saying they can work as low as 3-5 reps at up to 80-85% 1RM provided 3-4 reps in reserve are kept. Sims frames the same territory as ''maintain, don''t gain'' - continue resistance and cardiovascular training but train to preserve fitness, not improve it. One exercise scientist with 15+ years coaching pregnant clients says most can continue lifting heavy into the third trimester with few adjustments beyond avoiding painful prone positions and combat sports.','practice','moderate','{"note": "governing bodies 12-15 reps; GGS advanced 3-5 reps @80-85% 1RM, 3-4 RIR"}'::jsonb,'Eric Cressey - Molly Galbraith; Huberman Lab (Sims); Menno Henselmans'),
  ('female-cycle','pregnancy - exercise selection and contraindications (buildable exclusion list)','Galbraith''s contraindication list: high-intensity interval training, Valsalva, weight belts, horseback riding, scuba diving, contact sports (boxing, rugby), exercise in high heat (hot yoga/hot Pilates), and high altitude. Barbell Olympic lifts where the bar passes the midsection (cleans, snatches) are acceptable only in the first trimester and only for very experienced lifters whose belly has not yet protruded, since keeping the normal bar path risks fetal trauma while altering it risks maternal injury. Supine: reduces blood flow by about 50% during exercise, generally acceptable for about 60 seconds absent dizziness/tingling/numbness/lightheadedness, made safer by elevating head and shoulders to roughly a 15% incline; the literature does not clearly define a safe duration threshold, with cited onset figures ranging 16-28 weeks. CONFLICT: Sims says hot yoga at ~40?C is not considered detrimental provided the woman leaves or lies down if too hot, against the general heat contraindication.','practice','moderate','{"note": "supine ~50% flow reduction, ~60 s, ~15% incline, 16-28 week threshold conflict; hot yoga ~40?C/100?F"}'::jsonb,'Eric Cressey - Molly Galbraith; Huberman Lab (Sims)'),
  ('female-cycle','pregnancy - pelvic floor and diastasis prevalence (single-source, uncited)','Galbraith states 48-67% of women leak urine by week 30 of pregnancy; essentially 100% have diastasis recti by around week 35, which is not preventable and heals naturally to a degree, with the field''s standard for ''healed'' having shifted from closing the finger-breadth gap (old measure: under two finger-breadths) to generating tautness/tension in the linea alba, since gap closure alone did not correlate with restored core function. Postpartum: ~35% (one in three) report painful sex within 12 months, a possible sign of pelvic floor dysfunction; up to 19% (one in five) will have surgery for pelvic organ prolapse or incontinence by age 85, with 30% of those requiring multiple procedures. She also states 85% of US women will have a baby and are technically postpartum for life thereafter, and that 67-75% of people who hire a fitness coach are women. All practitioner figures without citations.','practice','weak','{"note": "48-67% leaking by wk 30; ~100% diastasis by wk 35; ~35% painful sex within 12 mo; up to 19% surgery by 85, 30% multiple; 85% become mothers; 67-75% of coaching clients are women"}'::jsonb,'Eric Cressey Physical Preparation Podcast - Molly Galbraith'),
  ('female-cycle','postpartum - concrete return-to-exercise timeline (most buildable artifact in this corpus)','Girls Gone Strong''s postpartum timeline for an uncomplicated birth: weeks 0-6 rehab/recovery - breathing, gentle walking building toward 10 minutes at a time, bodyweight movements no more strenuous than daily activities; weeks 6-18 (a 12-week return-to-exercise phase) - start at 2-3 sets of 8-10 reps across 4-6 exercises, progress to 2-3 sets of 10-12; weeks 18-42 (a 24-week phase) - heavier and more complex loading including barbell squats, plus gradual return to running from about week 18 (e.g. alternating 10-15 s jogging with 45-60 s walking). The timeline can be pushed LATER but never compressed: C-section, significant birth trauma, postpartum depression, poor sleep or notable pelvic floor dysfunction extend it, because tissue healing cannot be accelerated even though a program can be delayed. Separately, the sources note US women are typically NOT referred to pelvic-floor physical therapy even after a C-section (major abdominal surgery), whereas such referral is routine in parts of Europe - framed as a gap a coach or app should help bridge.','practice','moderate','{"note": "wk 0-6 / 6-18 / 18-42; 2-3?8-10 ? 2-3?10-12 across 4-6 exercises; running from wk 18, 10-15 s jog / 45-60 s walk"}'::jsonb,'Eric Cressey Physical Preparation Podcast - Molly Galbraith'),
  ('female-cycle','red_s - definition and threshold','Low energy availability is defined as roughly below 30 kcal per kg of fat-free mass per day, explicitly distinguished from an ordinary calorie deficit - Baraki states RED-S is not what most women on a normal weight-loss diet are falling into, and Colenso-Semple notes the standard research equations only account for EXERCISE energy expenditure and ignore the rest of the day, and that this is a concern for high-volume female athletes chronically underfueling over months to years, not a general-population concern. CONFLICT: Sims gives a sex-differentiated tipping point of ~15 kcal/kg FFM for men vs ~30 for women, implying a much narrower female margin; uncorroborated elsewhere in the corpus.','practice','moderate','{"note": "LEA ~30 kcal/kg FFM/day; Sims: 15 (men) vs 30 (women) kcal/kg FFM"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 4; Andy Galpin channel; Huberman Lab (Sims)'),
  ('female-cycle','red_s - the actionable warning sign and referral boundary','The earliest sign of low energy availability is menstrual cycle dysfunction or loss of period, with downstream effects including broader endocrine dysfunction, cardiovascular and GI issues, reduced muscle protein synthesis and loss of bone mineral density; menstrual recovery alone can take months even after load is reduced and intake increased, with particular concern for athletes not yet at peak bone mass (e.g. teenage gymnasts). Supporting: younger women with hypothalamic amenorrhea end up with lower bone density, and competitive cyclists in low energy availability showed DEXA-documented drops from normal to low bone density plus stress fractures. Sims frames cycle irregularity as warranting investigation of causes (PCOS, endometriosis, excessive load, poor sleep, travel, energy mismatch) rather than immediately prescribing hormonal contraception to restore bleeding, treats several years of absent periods as warranting an earlier-than-standard bone scan, and argues training INTENSITY itself is not the cause - underfueling is, with any deficit kept small (example 150-200 kcal) and timed away from training. NO source gives a numeric referral threshold (e.g. number of missed cycles) or a named pathway.','opinion','moderate','{"note": "example deficit 150-200 kcal; example amenorrhea duration cited: 7 years"}'::jsonb,'Andy Galpin channel (Colenso-Semple); Huberman Lab / Dr Stacy Sims channel panel'),
  ('female-cycle','red_s - cortisol claims are not supported','A 2021 meta-analysis (Dupuy/Dote and Montero; sample reported variously as ~155 and ~1,055 across batches, so treat n as unverified) found cortisol rises sharply but modestly during HIIT, drops below baseline 2-3 hours after, and returns to baseline by the next morning with no net day-over-day increase - resting cortisol ~300-350 nmol/L, HIIT peak ~600-700, versus Cushing''s syndrome continuously above 900. The CALERIE trial (220 adults, ~30% men / 70% women, 2 years of calorie deficit) found no significant between-group cortisol difference at 24 months despite substantial weight loss. Barbell Medicine calls ''100% false based on the available evidence'' the influencer claim that shorter, higher-intensity training harms menopausal women via cortisol. The Endocrine Society states no scientific proof supports ''adrenal fatigue'', and Cadegiani & Kater''s 2016 review of 58 studies concluded the construct does not exist - its symptom complex mapping instead onto depression, sleep disorders, hypothyroidism, anemia and perimenopause itself - with unnecessary adrenal supplements able to suppress endogenous HPA function and cause iatrogenic adrenal insufficiency persisting for months.','meta-analysis','strong','{"note": "resting ~300-350, HIIT peak ~600-700, Cushing''s >900 nmol/L; CALERIE n=220, 2 yrs, null; Cadegiani & Kater 58 studies"}'::jsonb,'Barbell Medicine - Menopause Parts 2 and 4');
INSERT INTO public.corpus_findings
  (area, topic, claim, basis, confidence, quantities, source)
VALUES
  ('female-cycle','perimenopause - timeline and diagnosis (soft, single-source)','Sims describes perimenopause as typically starting around age 35 and running to the average menopause age of 52 - a roughly 15-17 year window in which early symptoms (poor training adaptation, increased body fat, disrupted sleep, mood changes) are frequently misattributed to stress - and states there is no definitive blood test because hormone levels fluctuate too much for a single draw, so diagnosis relies on symptom tracking. She also states the first acceleration of bone loss occurs in the year or two before the final menstrual period. The age-35 onset figure is considerably earlier than typical clinical framing and is single-source; the SWAN bone data independently support the pre-final-menstrual-period loss acceleration.','practice','weak','{"note": "onset ~35, menopause ~52, span 15-17 yrs; bone loss acceleration ~1 yr before final menses"}'::jsonb,'Dr Stacy Sims - ''Why Women Gain Visceral Fat & Lose Muscle During Perimenopause'''),
  ('female-cycle','perimenopause - competing programming prescription (do NOT ship without flagging)','CONFLICT on the actual program. Sims recommends shifting perimenopausal training toward LESS overall volume and higher quality - not 90-minute sessions - favoring short high-intensity cardio or power-based resistance training ~3x/week plus cardio 2-4x/week, reasoning that only sufficiently intense stress triggers adaptations (insulin sensitivity, mitochondrial) that estrogen and progesterone previously supported; she separately argues heavy loading, not training to failure, restores the myosin isoform needed for power, and that lighter training to failure builds metabolically-driven mass without addressing estrogen-related power loss. Barbell Medicine''s evidence-based prescription is the standard adult one (2+ d/wk, 60-80% 1RM, 3-12 reps, RIR ~2, 150-300 min conditioning) with explicitly no menopause-specific protocol. The two overlap on ''lift heavy, keep sessions short'' but diverge sharply on whether volume should be cut and whether rep range matters differently for women.','opinion','weak','{"note": "Sims: RT ~3x/wk + cardio 2-4x/wk; BBM: 2+ d/wk, 60-80% 1RM, 3-12 reps"}'::jsonb,'Dr Stacy Sims channel vs Barbell Medicine - Menopause Part 4');
