-- Corpus sweep 2026-08-26 (second wave): optimal volume PER MUSCLE GROUP,
-- and female physiology / cycle-aware training. Owner-directed.
--
-- Both passes were asked to answer a DECISION, not just to gather, and both
-- came back rejecting the thing they were sent to support. That is the
-- valuable outcome and it is recorded here so the reasoning survives:
--
-- VOLUME: do NOT ship a hard per-session per-muscle cap. The only effect
-- shown to hurt growth is dropping BELOW 12 weekly sets per muscle. Capping
-- a 17-set chest day at 8 in a once-weekly split converts 17 weekly sets,
-- squarely inside the supported 12-20 band, into 8 - the one condition with
-- a demonstrated penalty. Every candidate cap is contested: 6 is unpublished
-- and biased by untrained subjects, 8 and 10 come from acute protein-
-- synthesis work (one in rats), 11 comes from a meta-analysis its own
-- presenter says was not controlled for weekly volume and whose threshold is
-- an admitted arbitrary statistical convention. Two studies once supporting
-- a session limit have been RETRACTED. Three RCTs found stacking neutral or
-- better.
--
-- FEMALE: offer a symptom-and-life-stage layer, NOT a cycle-phase engine.
-- The phase effect is absent in the best controlled data, the originator of
-- cycle syncing has retracted the blanket rules, and phase is not knowable
-- from a calendar. And the expected answer on perimenopause is wrong: a
-- 126-study meta-analysis finds peri- and postmenopausal women respond to
-- training like anyone else, so no menopause-specific PROTOCOL is warranted.
-- What is supported is life-stage EMPHASIS - heavy compound loading at
-- 65-85% 1RM because that is what moves bone, explicitly NOT softening load
-- for osteopenia, and balance work for the fall-rate reduction.





